<!--META-DATA
{
	"tags": ["monte-carlo", "probability", "dice", "simulation", "function"]
}
-->

<statement>
	<p>
		This function estimates the probability that the sum of rolling <m>N</m> fair six-sided dice equals a target value using Monte Carlo simulation.
	</p>

	<tabular valign="top" halign="right" top="minor">
		<col width="12%"/>
		<col width="88%" halign="left"/>

		<row>
			<cell><p>Inputs:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p><pf>N</pf> (1 x 1) integer — number of dice rolled per trial.</p>
				<p><pf>tar_sum</pf> (1 x 1) integer — desired total sum of the dice.</p>
				<p><pf>trials</pf> (1 x 1) integer — number of Monte Carlo trials to perform.</p>
				<p><pf>seed</pf> (optional, 1 x 1 integer) — random number generator seed for reproducibility.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>

		<row bottom="minor">
			<cell><p>Outputs:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p><pf>prob</pf> (1 x 1) double — estimated probability that the sum of <pf>N</pf> dice equals <pf>tar_sum</pf>.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell><p>Details:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p>▸ The simulation performs <pf>trials</pf> independent experiments. In each trial, <pf>N</pf> random integers between 1 and 6 are generated using <pf>randi(6,N,1)</pf> to represent the dice rolls.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell/>
			<cell><p>▸ The dice values are summed to compute <pf>roll_sum</pf>.</p></cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell/>
			<cell><p>▸ If <pf>roll_sum</pf> matches the desired <pf>tar_sum</pf>, the trial is counted as a "hit."</p></cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell/>
			<cell><p>▸ The estimated probability is computed as <m>\text{hits} / \text{trials}</m>.</p></cell>
		</row>

		<row bottom="minor">
			<cell><p>Notes:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p>▸ If <pf>seed</pf> is provided, the random number generator is initialized with <pf>rng(seed,'twister')</pf> for reproducible results.</p>
				<p>▸ The true probability can be computed analytically using the discrete convolution of <m>N</m> uniform discrete distributions on <m>\{1,2,3,4,5,6\}</m>, but this simulation provides an approximate result via repeated sampling.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>
	</tabular>

	<p>
		Examples:
		<program>
			<code>
				% Example 1: Estimate probability that 2 dice sum to 7
				prob = prob_die_roll_sum(2, 7, 10000)
				% returns a value close to 0.1667
			</code>
		</program>

		<program>
			<code>
				% Example 2: Reproducible simulation with fixed seed
				prob = prob_die_roll_sum(3, 10, 1e6, 21)
				% returns approximately 0.125
			</code>
		</program>
	</p>
</statement>
